# rules: php-missing-auth
# languages: php
# fixture: php/CWE-287b.php
# from: CWE-306 (Missing Authentication for Critical Function), CWE-287
#
# The rule reports an endpoint in a file that protects some of its others, and
# says nothing about a file that protects none. An application with no
# authentication anywhere has made a decision - the check may live in a filter,
# a gateway or a proxy, or it may be a sample with no accounts at all - and
# reporting every endpoint in it tells the reader nothing they did not know.
# What is a bug is the endpoint that was missed: a guard on three handlers and
# not on the fourth.
#
# There is one rule per language for this, and it reports under
# `<language>-missing-auth`. The corpus briefly carried two or three - an
# `-authn` spelling, an `-auth` spelling and this one - written against
# CWE-287, CWE-306 and CWE-862 but all asking the same question: is there an
# authentication guard on this endpoint. They fired on the same lines and a
# route with no middleware got three findings under three ids. Whether the
# missing guard is called authentication or authorization is not something the
# syntax says, so it is one finding; `<language>-862-rbac` is the separate rule
# for the endpoint that does authenticate and then checks no role.
#
# PHP endpoints that read $_GET/$_POST without first checking $_SESSION
# allow unauthenticated access. The rule flags direct use of superglobals
# where the access is not inside an isset($_SESSION) guard; safe code
# wraps superglobal access in if (isset($_SESSION['user'])).
#
# A PHP superglobal cannot be written into a pattern: `$_GET` is a `$` and a
# name, which is exactly how a hole is spelled, so `$_GET[$K]` reads as "any
# variable subscripted by anything" and matched every array access in the
# file. The name is pinned by guard on the capture instead - the capture text
# carries the `$` - and the same goes for `$_SESSION` in the guard patterns,
# where reading it as a hole made every `isset($arr[$k])` an authentication
# check. Over Guzzle, which has no session and no endpoint, the unpinned rule
# reported 971 lines.

["$V[$K]"] as $sinks
| ["if (isset($S[$K])) { $$$BODY }", "if ($S[$K]) { $$$BODY }", "isset($S[$K])"] as $auth
| scan_ast(["*.php"]; $sinks + $auth) as $all
| ($all | of(["if (isset($S[$K])) { $$$BODY }", "if ($S[$K]) { $$$BODY }"])
    | where_capture("S"; "^\\$_SESSION$")) as $guards
| ( $all | of($sinks)
      | where_capture("V"; "^\\$_(?:GET|POST|REQUEST)$")
      | outside($guards)
      | in_files_with($guards)
    | finding("php-missing-auth";
        "Superglobal $_GET/$_POST used without session check; add isset($_SESSION['user']) guard") )
| report
